Z||000015||Taijiguy||04-04-2001||08:08 AM||||This is the danger of dealing within such a small community. Obviously, there *are* always two sides to every story. As a business person myself, I have mixed feelings about El's response (or non-response) What's worse? To just sit by and be dragged through the mud, and wait for it to blow over, or do you paint a picture of the customer that's uglier than the one they are painting of you? I can't say I know how I would handle this situation. Lord knows, I get damn pissed if I don't get what I feel I paid for. I think this is mostly a lesson in economics: If the job ain't done, don't pay for it. I've had clients in the past who would withold part of a final payment until I was able to complete the work. I didn't like it worth a damn, but I always finished the job. Usually found a way to do it sooner than I thought I could. It just takes proper motivation.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||Dingo||04-04-2001||10:28 PM||||I didnt say 2000 USd wasnt a lot of money. I said when I got hosed over it wasn't for that amount of money. <P>It sucks bad to get screwed by someone for any amount of money. First thing one should always do is ask for references and then speak with those people. Ask to see the work done and if they liked the work performed and  did they need to bring it back for adjustments or repairs and if so how the shop dealt with those issues. Some shops have photo albums of the work they do.<P>||||reg||12
